be taken consecutively or divided into two-week blocks'.\",\"evidence\":\"may be taken consecutively or divided into two-week blocks within the child's first year.\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q23\",\"questionNumber\":23,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"welfare\",\"estimatedBand\":6,\"prompt\":\"Returning mothers may work 80% hours for full salary during their first three months back.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Section 2A confirms mothers can work eighty percent hours for one hundred percent salary for three months.\",\"evidence\":\"work eighty percent of their contracted hours for the first three months while receiving one hundred percent of their salary.\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q24\",\"questionNumber\":24,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"facilities\",\"estimatedBand\":6.5,\"prompt\":\"Nursing suites include medical-grade refrigerators for breast milk storage.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Section 2A mentions private nursing suites equipped with hospital-grade refrigeration units.\",\"evidence\":\"equipped with hospital-grade refrigeration units and ergonomic seating\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q25\",\"questionNumber\":25,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"safety\",\"estimatedBand\":6,\"prompt\":\"Passenger elevators may be used during a fire evacuation if an employee is disabled.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"FALSE\",\"explanation\":\"Section 2B states lifts are locked in the lobby during alarms and must never be used.\",\"evidence\":\"passenger lifts are automatically locked in the lobby during alarms and must never be used.\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q26\",\"questionNumber\":26,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"equipment\",\"estimatedBand\":6.5,\"prompt\":\"Defibrillators (AEDs) are situated next to passenger elevators on each floor.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"TRUE\",\"explanation\":\"Section 2B states AEDs and kits are mounted adjacent to primary passenger lifts on every floor.\",\"evidence\":\"mounted on every floor adjacent to the primary passenger lifts.\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q27\",\"questionNumber\":27,\"type\":\"true_false_not_given\",\"topic\":\"fire safety\",\"estimatedBand\":6.5,\"prompt\":\"Staff who leave the assembly point early are subject to disciplinary action.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"NOT GIVEN\",\"explanation\":\"Section 2B instructs staff to remain until the all-clear is given, but does not mention specific disciplinary penalties.\",\"evidence\":\"Employees must remain at the assembly point until the Chief Fire Safety Officer issues an official all-clear signal.\"}]}]},{\"passageNumber\":3,\"title\":\"Urban Wildlife Adaptation: How Animals Thrive in Modern Cities\",\"subtitle\":\"The evolutionary, behavioral, and physiological transformations of metropolitan animals\",\"text\":\"Paragraph A\\nFor centuries, human cities were characterized by naturalists as ecological wastelands—concrete deserts hostile to all biological life except domestic pets and unwanted vermin like brown rats and feral pigeons. However, evolutionary biologists are discovering that twenty-first-century metropolises represent dynamic crucibles of rapid natural selection. Driven by vast food abundance, reduced apex predator threats, and novel environmental challenges, dozens of wild species are not merely surviving in cities; they are undergoing accelerated anatomical and behavioral adaptations to thrive alongside millions of human beings.\\n\\nParagraph B\\nA magnificent exemplar of urban avian colonization is the peregrine falcon (Falco peregrinus). Historically nesting on remote coastal sea cliffs and high alpine crags, the peregrine falcon was driven to near-extinction in the 1960s by organochlorine pesticides like DDT. Today, the world's densest nesting concentrations of peregrines are found not in national parks, but in urban metropolises such as London, New York, and Melbourne. Concrete and steel skyscrapers mimic vertical natural cliffs, offering secure nesting ledges immune to ground predators. Furthermore, artificial municipal lighting allows falcons to hunt nocturnally, preying on abundant urban pigeon populations and migrating songbirds drawn into city centers by skyscraper glow.\\n\\nParagraph C\\nIn terrestrial mammals, the red fox (Vulpes vulpes) in British cities illustrates remarkable behavioral and morphological shifts. Genetic sequencing conducted by the University of Glasgow revealed that urban foxes are diverging genetically from their rural counterparts. Urban foxes have evolved shorter, wider snouts with stronger bite forces, adapted for crunching through discarded plastic packaging and fast-food chicken bones. Furthermore, their brains exhibit reduced reactivity to startling loud noises and sudden human movements—a behavioral syndrome known as self-domestication, mirroring the early evolutionary path of ancestral wolves transitioning into domestic dogs.\\n\\nParagraph D\\nIn Australia, the grey-headed flying fox (Pteropus poliocephalus)—a giant fruit bat—has established permanent multi-thousand-strong urban camps along the Yarra River in Melbourne and the Parramatta River in Sydney. While historically nomadic, following seasonal forest flowering, urban bats now remain year-round because municipal botanical parks and exotic garden plantings provide an uninterrupted supply of flowering eucalyptus, figs, and decorative fruit trees throughout winter months.\\n\\nParagraph E\\nHowever, urban adaptation is not without severe physiological hazards. Constant exposure to artificial light at night (ALAN) suppresses the biological production of melatonin in urban birds, disrupting circadian sleep cycles and causing songbirds to sing at two in the morning. Furthermore, diets rich in processed human junk food—such as greasy french fries and sugary pastries—have induced high rates of avian atherosclerosis and fatty liver disease in urban crows and gulls. Ultimately, while cities demonstrate nature's astonishing evolutionary plasticity, ensuring long-term urban biodiversity will require intentional green architecture, dark-sky lighting corridors, and expanded wildlife corridors connecting fragmented urban parks to surrounding countryside.\",\"wordCount\":520,\"questionGroups\":[{\"groupInstruction\":\"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C, or D. Write the correct letter in boxes 28–33 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"g5-q28\",\"questionNumber\":28,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"biology\",\"estimatedBand\":6,\"prompt\":\"In the mid-twentieth century, peregrine falcons nearly disappeared because of\",\"options\":[\"the widespread agricultural use of DDT.\",\"loss of food due to harsh winters.\",\"hunting by domestic cats.\",\"collisions with early aircraft.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains falcons were driven to near-extinction by organochlorine pesticides like DDT.\",\"evidence\":\"driven to near-extinction in the 1960s by organochlorine pesticides like DDT.\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q29\",\"questionNumber\":29,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"biology\",\"estimatedBand\":6.5,\"prompt\":\"Skyscrapers provide an ideal urban habitat for peregrine falcons because they\",\"options\":[\"feature wooden roof structures shielded from rain.\",\"resemble vertical cliff faces offering secure nesting ledges.\",\"house indoor pigeon colonies that ensure constant food.\",\"generate artificial heat that keeps them warm in winter.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"B\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B states skyscrapers mimic vertical natural cliffs, offering ledges immune to predators.\",\"evidence\":\"skyscrapers mimic vertical natural cliffs, offering secure nesting ledges immune to ground predators.\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q30\",\"questionNumber\":30,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"evolution\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Urban foxes in Glasgow have developed shorter, wider snouts to\",\"options\":[\"hunt fast underground moles.\",\"swim across urban canals.\",\"bite through food packaging and bones.\",\"fit into narrow sewer pipes.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"C\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C notes snouts with stronger bite forces adapted for crunching discarded packaging and bones.\",\"evidence\":\"shorter, wider snouts with stronger bite forces, adapted for crunching through discarded plastic packaging and fast-food chicken bones.\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q31\",\"questionNumber\":31,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"zoology\",\"estimatedBand\":6.5,\"prompt\":\"Grey-headed flying foxes in Melbourne have stopped migrating because\",\"options\":[\"they lost their ability to fly long distances.\",\"city streetlights frighten them from leaving.\",\"rural forests were completely cleared.\",\"parks and urban gardens supply flowers and fruit all year.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"D\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D states urban botanical parks and exotic plantings provide uninterrupted food throughout winter.\",\"evidence\":\"botanical parks and exotic garden plantings provide an uninterrupted supply of flowering eucalyptus, figs, and decorative fruit trees\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q32\",\"questionNumber\":32,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"health\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Artificial light at night harms urban birds by\",\"options\":[\"suppressing the hormone melatonin and disturbing sleep cycles.\",\"burning their feathers on lamppost fixtures.\",\"making nocturnally active insects impossible to locate.\",\"destroying their delicate retinal photoreceptors permanently.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"A\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E states light suppresses melatonin, disrupting circadian sleep cycles.\",\"evidence\":\"suppresses the biological production of melatonin in urban birds, disrupting circadian sleep cycles\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q33\",\"questionNumber\":33,\"type\":\"multiple_choice_single\",\"topic\":\"nutrition\",\"estimatedBand\":6.5,\"prompt\":\"Feeding on discarded fast food causes urban crows and gulls to suffer from\",\"options\":[\"broken beaks.\",\"cardiovascular and liver diseases.\",\"loss of flight feathers.\",\"contagious viral infections.\"],\"correctAnswer\":\"B\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E mentions high rates of avian atherosclerosis and fatty liver disease.\",\"evidence\":\"induced high rates of avian atherosclerosis and fatty liver disease in urban crows and gulls.\"}]},{\"groupInstruction\":\"Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 34–40 on your answer sheet.\",\"questionType\":\"summary_completion\",\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"g5-q34\",\"questionNumber\":34,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"adaptation\",\"estimatedBand\":6.5,\"prompt\":\"Artificial lighting allows peregrine falcons to hunt ____ during nighttime.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"nocturnally\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph B explains lighting allows falcons to hunt nocturnally.\",\"evidence\":\"artificial municipal lighting allows falcons to hunt nocturnally\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q35\",\"questionNumber\":35,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"zoology\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"The tolerance of loud noises in urban foxes is termed self-____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"domestication\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph C introduces the behavioral syndrome known as self-domestication.\",\"evidence\":\"behavioral syndrome known as self-domestication, mirroring the early evolutionary path\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q36\",\"questionNumber\":36,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"zoology\",\"estimatedBand\":6.5,\"prompt\":\"The Australian flying fox is biologically classified as a giant ____ bat.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"fruit\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D states the flying fox is a giant fruit bat.\",\"evidence\":\"grey-headed flying fox (Pteropus poliocephalus)—a giant fruit bat\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q37\",\"questionNumber\":37,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"biology\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Bats feed continuously on winter-flowering ____ and fig trees in cities.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"eucalyptus\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph D mentions flowering eucalyptus, figs, and decorative fruit trees.\",\"evidence\":\"flowering eucalyptus, figs, and decorative fruit trees throughout winter months.\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q38\",\"questionNumber\":38,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"health\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Artificial light suppresses production of the sleep hormone ____.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"melatonin\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E refers to biological production of melatonin.\",\"evidence\":\"suppresses the biological production of melatonin in urban birds\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q39\",\"questionNumber\":39,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"health\",\"estimatedBand\":7.5,\"prompt\":\"Junk food diets cause fatty ____ disease in crows and gulls.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"liver\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E notes atherosclerosis and fatty liver disease.\",\"evidence\":\"induced high rates of avian atherosclerosis and fatty liver disease in urban crows and gulls.\"},{\"id\":\"g5-q40\",\"questionNumber\":40,\"type\":\"summary_completion\",\"topic\":\"conservation\",\"estimatedBand\":7,\"prompt\":\"Planners recommend establishing dark-sky lighting ____ to protect night wildlife.\",\"correctAnswer\":\"corridors\",\"explanation\":\"Paragraph E highlights dark-sky lighting corridors.\",\"evidence\":\"dark-sky lighting corridors, and expanded wildlife corridors connecting fragmented urban parks\"}]}]}]");
